an athlete improves, the same session creates a smaller stimulus, so training must change through load, complexity, speed, opposition, specificity, or recovery. Progress is not linear, and periods of consolidation are part of development rather than evidence of failure.\n\n## Technique, tactics, and context\n\nTechnical actions such as first touch, passing weight, body orientation, scanning, tackling, and finishing determine whether a tactical idea can be executed at match speed.\n\nStrategic choices include pressing height, defensive line, overloads, width, rest defence, set-piece plans, and how aggressively a team commits players forward.\n\nThat distinction matters because two athletes can perform the same visible action for different reasons. Context changes the value of a decision. Score, time remaining, field position, fatigue, opponent behaviour, weather, equipment, competition format, and risk tolerance can all alter what the best choice looks like.\n\n## Measuring progress without oversimplifying it\n\nUseful measures include territory, shot quality, possession value, pressing success, transition frequency, chance creation, defensive compactness, and the context in which events occur.\n\nPerformance analysis should therefore combine outcome measures with process measures. Winning a possession, race, rally, or round is important, but coaches also need to know whether the underlying actions are becoming more repeatable. Good measurement reduces uncertainty without pretending that sport is fully predictable.\n\n## How training develops\n\nTraining progresses from isolated technique to opposed drills, constrained games, phase-specific rehearsals, and full match scenarios with representative pressure.\n\nTraining should become more representative as skill improves. Early practice often simplifies the task so the athlete can learn a movement or rule. Later practice introduces pressure, variability, tactical choices, time constraints, fatigue, and opponents. The goal is transfer: the athlete should be able to use the skill in competition rather than only reproduce it in a drill.\n\nRecovery is part of that process. Adaptation occurs when training stress is followed by enough restoration for the athlete to absorb it. More work is not automatically better work, especially when technique deteriorates or fatigue begins to hide the quality coaches are trying to develop.\n\n## Rules, safety, and fair competition\n\nLaws on dangerous play, concussion management, equipment, officiating, and field conditions are designed to reduce avoidable risk while preserving competitive play.\n\nSport also depends on integrity. Officials, governing bodies, coaches, event organisers, and athletes share responsibility for applying rules consistently, protecting participants, and maintaining conditions in which performance rather than avoidable hazard decides the outcome.\n\n## What to remember\n\nFootball tactics improves when athletes and coaches connect technique, tactical context, physical preparation, measurement, and recovery instead of treating them as separate subjects. The strongest training environments make objectives explicit, use rules and safety standards seriously, and evaluate whether practice is transferring to competition.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel sports explainer. Competition rules and technical regulations can change, so the relevant governing body's current rulebook should be checked when a decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/sports-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Football tactics: How training adapts over time","word_count":615,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["sports","evergreen","explainer","Football tactics"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"FIFA","title":"FIFA","url":"https://www.fifa.com/technical","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"IOC","title":"International Olympic Committee","url":"https://olympics.com/ioc","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization - Physical activity","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"sports-archive-2026-007","published_at":"2026-02-03T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"football-tactics-what-rules-and-safety-standards-protect-007","title":"Football tactics: What rules and safety standards protect","description":"Football tactics explained through what rules and safety standards protect, with technique, strategy, measurement, training progression, and safety context.","body_markdown":"# Football tactics: What rules and safety standards protect\n\nFootball tactics is best understood as a system of skill, decision-making, physical preparation, rules, and competitive context. This article focuses on what rules and safety standards protect while keeping the wider performance environment visible.\n\n## The performance system\n\nFootball tactics organise how a team creates space, protects space, progresses the ball, presses, defends transitions, and coordinates players across phases of possession.\n\nA useful way to analyse sport is to separate what the athlete can control directly from what is constrained by opponents, equipment, environment, and rules. Physical capacity creates options, technical skill makes those options usable, and decision-making determines which option is selected at a particular moment.\n\n## What rules and safety standards protect\n\nLaws on dangerous play, concussion management, equipment, officiating, and field conditions are designed to reduce avoidable risk while preserving competitive play. Rules do more than decide winners. They define acceptable contact, equipment, venue conditions, medical responsibilities, and procedural fairness. Strong safety systems combine prevention, trained supervision, emergency response, and a culture in which athletes can report problems without penalty.\n\n## Technique, tactics, and context\n\nTechnical actions such as first touch, passing weight, body orientation, scanning, tackling, and finishing determine whether a tactical idea can be executed at match speed.\n\nStrategic choices include pressing height, defensive line, overloads, width, rest defence, set-piece plans, and how aggressively a team commits players forward.\n\nThat distinction matters because two athletes can perform the same visible action for different reasons. Context changes the value of a decision. Score, time remaining, field position, fatigue, opponent behaviour, weather, equipment, competition format, and risk tolerance can all alter what the best choice looks like.\n\n## Measuring progress without oversimplifying it\n\nUseful measures include territory, shot quality, possession value, pressing success, transition frequency, chance creation, defensive compactness, and the context in which events occur.\n\nPerformance analysis should therefore combine outcome measures with process measures. Winning a possession, race, rally, or round is important, but coaches also need to know whether the underlying actions are becoming more repeatable. Good measurement reduces uncertainty without pretending that sport is fully predictable.\n\n## How training develops\n\nTraining progresses from isolated technique to opposed drills, constrained games, phase-specific rehearsals, and full match scenarios with representative pressure.\n\nTraining should become more representative as skill improves. Early practice often simplifies the task so the athlete can learn a movement or rule. Later practice introduces pressure, variability, tactical choices, time constraints, fatigue, and opponents. The goal is transfer: the athlete should be able to use the skill in competition rather than only reproduce it in a drill.\n\nRecovery is part of that process. Adaptation occurs when training stress is followed by enough restoration for the athlete to absorb it. More work is not automatically better work, especially when technique deteriorates or fatigue begins to hide the quality coaches are trying to develop.\n\n## Rules, safety, and fair competition\n\nLaws on dangerous play, concussion management, equipment, officiating, and field conditions are designed to reduce avoidable risk while preserving competitive play.\n\nSport also depends on integrity. Officials, governing bodies, coaches, event organisers, and athletes share responsibility for applying rules consistently, protecting participants, and maintaining conditions in which performance rather than avoidable hazard decides the outcome.\n\n## What to remember\n\nFootball tactics improves when athletes and coaches connect technique, tactical context, physical preparation, measurement, and recovery instead of treating them as separate subjects. The strongest training environments make objectives explicit, use rules and safety standards seriously, and evaluate whether practice is transferring to competition.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel sports explainer. Competition rules and technical regulations can change, so the relevant governing body's current rulebook should be checked when a decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/sports-fallback-3.png","featured_image_alt":"Football tactics: What rules and safety standards protect","word_count":618,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["sports","evergreen","explainer","Football tactics"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"FIFA","title":"FIFA","url":"https://www.fifa.com/technical","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"IOC","title":"International Olympic Committee","url":"https://olympics.com/ioc","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization - Physical activity","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"sports-archive-2026-008","published_at":"2026-02-04T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"rugby-laws-how-it-works-008","title":"Rugby laws: How it works","description":"Rugby laws explained through how it works, with technique, strategy, measurement, training progression, and safety context.","body_markdown":"# Rugby laws: How it works\n\nRugby laws is best understood as a system of skill, decision-making, physical preparation, rules, and competitive context. This article focuses on how it works while keeping the wider performance environment visible.\n\n## The performance system\n\nRugby laws define how possession changes, how contact is managed, how territory is gained, and how contests such as tackles, rucks, mauls, scrums, and lineouts are regulated.\n\nA useful way to analyse sport is to separate what the athlete can control directly from what is constrained by opponents, equipment, environment, and rules. Physical capacity creates options, technical skill makes those options usable, and decision-making determines which option is selected at a particular moment.\n\n## How it works\n\nRugby laws define how possession changes, how contact is managed, how territory is gained, and how contests such as tackles, rucks, mauls, scrums, and lineouts are regulated. The system works because individual actions are connected to a larger structure: technique produces options, decisions select among those options, and rules define which outcomes are legal. Strategy changes with field position, score, weather, numerical advantage, set-piece strength, kicking options, and whether a team wants tempo or control.\n\n## Technique, tactics, and context\n\nSafe and effective technique matters in tackling, body height, binding, passing, kicking, scrummaging, and support play because poor execution can lose possession or increase injury risk.\n\nStrategy changes with field position, score, weather, numerical advantage, set-piece strength, kicking options, and whether a team wants tempo or control.\n\nThat distinction matters because two athletes can perform the same visible action for different reasons. Context changes the value of a decision. Score, time remaining, field position, fatigue, opponent behaviour, weather, equipment, competition format, and risk tolerance can all alter what the best choice looks like.\n\n## Measuring progress without oversimplifying it\n\nPerformance can be assessed through gain-line success, tackle completion, ruck speed, territory, possession retention, kicking outcomes, set-piece efficiency, and discipline.\n\nPerformance analysis should therefore combine outcome measures with process measures. Winning a possession, race, rally, or round is important, but coaches also need to know whether the underlying actions are becoming more repeatable. Good measurement reduces uncertainty without pretending that sport is fully predictable.\n\n## How training develops\n\nPlayers progress from law understanding and controlled contact to unit work, conditioned games, decision-making under fatigue, and match-intensity contact where appropriate.\n\nTraining should become more representative as skill improves. Early practice often simplifies the task so the athlete can learn a movement or rule. Later practice introduces pressure, variability, tactical choices, time constraints, fatigue, and opponents. The goal is transfer: the athlete should be able to use the skill in competition rather than only reproduce it in a drill.\n\nRecovery is part of that process. Adaptation occurs when training stress is followed by enough restoration for the athlete to absorb it. More work is not automatically better work, especially when technique deteriorates or fatigue begins to hide the quality coaches are trying to develop.\n\n## Rules, safety, and fair competition\n\nHigh-contact laws, head-contact sanctions, scrum engagement requirements, medical protocols, and equipment standards are central to player welfare.\n\nSport also depends on integrity. Officials, governing bodies, coaches, event organisers, and athletes share responsibility for applying rules consistently, protecting participants, and maintaining conditions in which performance rather than avoidable hazard decides the outcome.\n\n## What to remember\n\nRugby laws improves when athletes and coaches connect technique, tactical context, physical preparation, measurement, and recovery instead of treating them as separate subjects. The strongest training environments make objectives explicit, use rules and safety standards seriously, and evaluate whether practice is transferring to competition.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel sports explainer. Competition rules and technical regulations can change, so the relevant governing body's current rulebook should be checked when a decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/sports-fallback-4.png","featured_image_alt":"Rugby laws: How it works","word_count":625,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["sports","evergreen","explainer","Rugby laws"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"World Rugby","title":"World Rugby","url":"https://www.world.rugby/the-game/laws/home","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"IOC","title":"International Olympic Committee","url":"https://olympics.com/ioc","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization - Physical activity","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"sports-archive-2026-009","published_at":"2026-02-05T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"rugby-laws-why-technique-matters-009","title":"Rugby laws: Why technique matters","description":"Rugby laws explained through why technique matters, with technique, strategy, measurement, training progression, and safety context.","body_markdown":"# Rugby laws: Why technique matters\n\nRugby laws is best understood as a system of skill, decision-making, physical preparation, rules, and competitive context. This article focuses on why technique matters while keeping the wider performance environment visible.\n\n## The performance system\n\nRugby laws define how possession changes, how contact is managed, how territory is gained, and how contests such as tackles, rucks, mauls, scrums, and lineouts are regulated.\n\nA useful way to analyse sport is to separate what the athlete can control directly from what is constrained by opponents, equipment, environment, and rules. Physical capacity creates options, technical skill makes those options usable, and decision-making determines which option is selected at a particular moment.\n\n## Why technique matters\n\nSafe and effective technique matters in tackling, body height, binding, passing, kicking, scrummaging, and support play because poor execution can lose possession or increase injury risk. Technique matters most when it remains reliable under speed, fatigue, uncertainty, and pressure. It is therefore better understood as repeatable problem-solving than as a perfectly fixed visual style.\n\n## Technique, tactics, and context\n\nSafe and effective technique matters in tackling, body height, binding, passing, kicking, scrummaging, and support play because poor execution can lose possession or increase injury risk.\n\nStrategy changes with field position, score, weather, numerical advantage, set-piece strength, kicking options, and whether a team wants tempo or control.\n\nThat distinction matters because two athletes can perform the same visible action for different reasons. Context changes the value of a decision. Score, time remaining, field position, fatigue, opponent behaviour, weather, equipment, competition format, and risk tolerance can all alter what the best choice looks like.\n\n## Measuring progress without oversimplifying it\n\nPerformance can be assessed through gain-line success, tackle completion, ruck speed, territory, possession retention, kicking outcomes, set-piece efficiency, and discipline.\n\nPerformance analysis should therefore combine outcome measures with process measures. Winning a possession, race, rally, or round is important, but coaches also need to know whether the underlying actions are becoming more repeatable. Good measurement reduces uncertainty without pretending that sport is fully predictable.\n\n## How training develops\n\nPlayers progress from law understanding and controlled contact to unit work, conditioned games, decision-making under fatigue, and match-intensity contact where appropriate.\n\nTraining should become more representative as skill improves. Early practice often simplifies the task so the athlete can learn a movement or rule. Later practice introduces pressure, variability, tactical choices, time constraints, fatigue, and opponents. The goal is transfer: the athlete should be able to use the skill in competition rather than only reproduce it in a drill.\n\nRecovery is part of that process. Adaptation occurs when training stress is followed by enough restoration for the athlete to absorb it. More work is not automatically better work, especially when technique deteriorates or fatigue begins to hide the quality coaches are trying to develop.\n\n## Rules, safety, and fair competition\n\nHigh-contact laws, head-contact sanctions, scrum engagement requirements, medical protocols, and equipment standards are central to player welfare.\n\nSport also depends on integrity. Officials, governing bodies, coaches, event organisers, and athletes share responsibility for applying rules consistently, protecting participants, and maintaining conditions in which performance rather than avoidable hazard decides the outcome.\n\n## What to remember\n\nRugby laws improves when athletes and coaches connect technique, tactical context, physical preparation, measurement, and recovery instead of treating them as separate subjects. The strongest training environments make objectives explicit, use rules and safety standards seriously, and evaluate whether practice is transferring to competition.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel sports explainer. Competition rules and technical regulations can change, so the relevant governing body's current rulebook should be checked when a decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/sports-fallback-1.png","featured_image_alt":"Rugby laws: Why technique matters","word_count":604,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["sports","evergreen","explainer","Rugby laws"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"World Rugby","title":"World Rugby","url":"https://www.world.rugby/the-game/laws/home","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"IOC","title":"International Olympic Committee","url":"https://olympics.com/ioc","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization - Physical activity","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"sports-archive-2026-010","published_at":"2026-02-06T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"rugby-laws-where-strategy-changes-outcomes-010","title":"Rugby laws: Where strategy changes outcomes","description":"Rugby laws explained through where strategy changes outcomes, with technique, strategy, measurement, training progression, and safety context.","body_markdown":"# Rugby laws: Where strategy changes outcomes\n\nRugby laws is best understood as a system of skill, decision-making, physical preparation, rules, and competitive context. This article focuses on where strategy changes outcomes while keeping the wider performance environment visible.\n\n## The performance system\n\nRugby laws define how possession changes, how contact is managed, how territory is gained, and how contests such as tackles, rucks, mauls, scrums, and lineouts are regulated.\n\nA useful way to analyse sport is to separate what the athlete can control directly from what is constrained by opponents, equipment, environment, and rules. Physical capacity creates options, technical skill makes those options usable, and decision-making determines which option is selected at a particular moment.\n\n## Where strategy changes outcomes\n\nStrategy changes with field position, score, weather, numerical advantage, set-piece strength, kicking options, and whether a team wants tempo or control. Strategy matters because athletes and teams operate with limited time, energy, information, and risk tolerance. A good plan creates favourable probabilities, but it still depends on execution and adaptation when the opponent or environment changes.\n\n## Technique, tactics, and context\n\nSafe and effective technique matters in tackling, body height, binding, passing, kicking, scrummaging, and support play because poor execution can lose possession or increase injury risk.\n\nStrategy changes with field position, score, weather, numerical advantage, set-piece strength, kicking options, and whether a team wants tempo or control.\n\nThat distinction matters because two athletes can perform the same visible action for different reasons. Context changes the value of a decision. Score, time remaining, field position, fatigue, opponent behaviour, weather, equipment, competition format, and risk tolerance can all alter what the best choice looks like.\n\n## Measuring progress without oversimplifying it\n\nPerformance can be assessed through gain-line success, tackle completion, ruck speed, territory, possession retention, kicking outcomes, set-piece efficiency, and discipline.\n\nPerformance analysis should therefore combine outcome measures with process measures. Winning a possession, race, rally, or round is important, but coaches also need to know whether the underlying actions are becoming more repeatable. Good measurement reduces uncertainty without pretending that sport is fully predictable.\n\n## How training develops\n\nPlayers progress from law understanding and controlled contact to unit work, conditioned games, decision-making under fatigue, and match-intensity contact where appropriate.\n\nTraining should become more representative as skill improves. Early practice often simplifies the task so the athlete can learn a movement or rule. Later practice introduces pressure, variability, tactical choices, time constraints, fatigue, and opponents. The goal is transfer: the athlete should be able to use the skill in competition rather than only reproduce it in a drill.\n\nRecovery is part of that process. Adaptation occurs when training stress is followed by enough restoration for the athlete to absorb it. More work is not automatically better work, especially when technique deteriorates or fatigue begins to hide the quality coaches are trying to develop.\n\n## Rules, safety, and fair competition\n\nHigh-contact laws, head-contact sanctions, scrum engagement requirements, medical protocols, and equipment standards are central to player welfare.\n\nSport also depends on integrity. Officials, governing bodies, coaches, event organisers, and athletes share responsibility for applying rules consistently, protecting participants, and maintaining conditions in which performance rather than avoidable hazard decides the outcome.\n\n## What to remember\n\nRugby laws improves when athletes and coaches connect technique, tactical context, physical preparation, measurement, and recovery instead of treating them as separate subjects. The strongest training environments make objectives explicit, use rules and safety standards seriously, and evaluate whether practice is transferring to competition.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel sports explainer. Competition rules and technical regulations can change, so the relevant governing body's current rulebook should be checked when a decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/sports-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Rugby laws: Where strategy changes outcomes","word_count":609,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["sports","evergreen","explainer","Rugby laws"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"World Rugby","title":"World Rugby","url":"https://www.world.rugby/the-game/laws/home","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"IOC","title":"International Olympic Committee","url":"https://olympics.com/ioc","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization - Physical activity","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"sports-archive-2026-011","published_at":"2026-02-07T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"rugby-laws-how-performance-is-measured-011","title":"Rugby laws: How performance is measured","description":"Rugby laws explained through how performance is measured, with technique, strategy, measurement, training progression, and safety context.","body_markdown":"# Rugby laws: How performance is measured\n\nRugby laws is best understood as a system of skill, decision-making, physical preparation, rules, and competitive context. This article focuses on how performance is measured while keeping the wider performance environment visible.\n\n## The performance system\n\nRugby laws define how possession changes, how contact is managed, how territory is gained, and how contests such as tackles, rucks, mauls, scrums, and lineouts are regulated.\n\nA useful way to analyse sport is to separate what the athlete can control directly from what is constrained by opponents, equipment, environment, and rules. Physical capacity creates options, technical skill makes those options usable, and decision-making determines which option is selected at a particular moment.\n\n## How performance is measured\n\nPerformance can be assessed through gain-line success, tackle completion, ruck speed, territory, possession retention, kicking outcomes, set-piece efficiency, and discipline. Measurement is most useful when the metric is linked to the actual performance question. One number can describe an event without explaining why it happened, so video, context, opponent strength, role, and repeated observation often remain necessary.\n\n## Technique, tactics, and context\n\nSafe and effective technique matters in tackling, body height, binding, passing, kicking, scrummaging, and support play because poor execution can lose possession or increase injury risk.\n\nStrategy changes with field position, score, weather, numerical advantage, set-piece strength, kicking options, and whether a team wants tempo or control.\n\nThat distinction matters because two athletes can perform the same visible action for different reasons. Context changes the value of a decision. Score, time remaining, field position, fatigue, opponent behaviour, weather, equipment, competition format, and risk tolerance can all alter what the best choice looks like.\n\n## Measuring progress without oversimplifying it\n\nPerformance can be assessed through gain-line success, tackle completion, ruck speed, territory, possession retention, kicking outcomes, set-piece efficiency, and discipline.\n\nPerformance analysis should therefore combine outcome measures with process measures. Winning a possession, race, rally, or round is important, but coaches also need to know whether the underlying actions are becoming more repeatable. Good measurement reduces uncertainty without pretending that sport is fully predictable.\n\n## How training develops\n\nPlayers progress from law understanding and controlled contact to unit work, conditioned games, decision-making under fatigue, and match-intensity contact where appropriate.\n\nTraining should become more representative as skill improves. Early practice often simplifies the task so the athlete can learn a movement or rule. Later practice introduces pressure, variability, tactical choices, time constraints, fatigue, and opponents. The goal is transfer: the athlete should be able to use the skill in competition rather than only reproduce it in a drill.\n\nRecovery is part of that process. Adaptation occurs when training stress is followed by enough restoration for the athlete to absorb it. More work is not automatically better work, especially when technique deteriorates or fatigue begins to hide the quality coaches are trying to develop.\n\n## Rules, safety, and fair competition\n\nHigh-contact laws, head-contact sanctions, scrum engagement requirements, medical protocols, and equipment standards are central to player welfare.\n\nSport also depends on integrity. Officials, governing bodies, coaches, event organisers, and athletes share responsibility for applying rules consistently, protecting participants, and maintaining conditions in which performance rather than avoidable hazard decides the outcome.\n\n## What to remember\n\nRugby laws improves when athletes and coaches connect technique, tactical context, physical preparation, measurement, and recovery instead of treating them as separate subjects. The strongest training environments make objectives explicit, use rules and safety standards seriously, and evaluate whether practice is transferring to competition.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel sports explainer. Competition rules and technical regulations can change, so the relevant governing body's current rulebook should be checked when a decision depends on exact wording.","